      "math_academy_id": "q-103668",
      "knowledge_point_id": "744f58f2-59be-552a-bb2f-d0819f35c1ca",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Vertex of a Vertical Parabola by Completing the Square",
      "problem": "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}+2x-2y-1=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $x$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-2y-1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =2y+1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}-1 & =2y+1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =2y+1+1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =2y+2 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k)=(-1,-1).$",
